What is Spring Security?
Spring Security is a powerful framework that provides:
Authentication → verifying who the user is
Authorization → verifying what the user is allowed to do

It integrates seamlessly with Spring Boot and works by configuring filters that intercept every HTTP request
 before reaching your controllers.
 
 | Concept                | Description                                               | Example                                                                 |
| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Authentication**     | Verifies identity (username/password, token, OAuth, etc.) | Logging in with user credentials                                        |
| **Authorization**      | Checks permissions (roles or privileges)                  | Allow only ADMINs to delete data                                        |
| **Principal**          | The current logged-in user                                | `SecurityContextHolder.getContext().getAuthentication().getPrincipal()` |
| **SecurityContext**    | Stores authentication info for the current request/thread | Used internally by Spring                                               |
| **FilterChain**        | A chain of security filters that process each request     | JWT filter, UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter                        |
| **UserDetailsService** | Loads user info from DB or memory                         | Custom user repository                                                  |
| **PasswordEncoder**    | Hashes passwords securely (BCrypt recommended)            | `new BCryptPasswordEncoder()`                                           |
 
Spring Security Workflow:
Client → Security Filter Chain → AuthenticationManager → UserDetailsService → PasswordEncoder
           ↓
         Success → SecurityContext → Controller
           ↓
         Failure → 401 Unauthorized

Explanation:
A request hits your app.
The Security Filter Chain intercepts it.
The framework checks whether the endpoint requires authentication.
If yes, it uses the AuthenticationManager to validate credentials.
The UserDetailsService loads user details (from memory or DB).
PasswordEncoder validates the password.
If valid, authentication success → user details stored in SecurityContext.
Controller executes normally.
On logout or token expiry, the context is cleared. 

@EnableMethodSecurity --> used to activate method-level security in a Spring Boot application.
It allows you to use annotations like:
@PreAuthorize
@PostAuthorize
@Secured
@RolesAllowed

| Annotation       | Description                                                   | Example                                                       |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `@PreAuthorize`  | Checks permission **before** method executes                  | `@PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")`                           |
| `@PostAuthorize` | Checks permission **after** method executes (based on result) | `@PostAuthorize("returnObject.owner == authentication.name")` |
| `@Secured`       | Older style; allows role-based checks only                    | `@Secured("ROLE_ADMIN")`                                      |
| `@RolesAllowed`  | From Jakarta; same as `@Secured`                              | `@RolesAllowed("ADMIN")`                                      |


Authentication type in Spring Security:
| Type                                 | Description                                       | Use Case                        |
| ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| **In-memory**                        | Users stored directly in code (simple demo setup) | Demos, prototypes               |
| **JDBC Authentication**              | Users stored in database                          | Basic enterprise apps           |
| **LDAP Authentication**              | Auth through directory service                    | Enterprise environment          |
| **Custom Authentication**            | Use your own user service                         | When integrating with custom DB |
| **JWT / Token-based Authentication** | Stateless authentication using tokens             | Modern REST APIs, microservices |
| **OAuth2 / SSO**                     | External login (Google, Okta, Azure AD)           | Enterprise SSO                  |

Authorization levels:
| Type             | Description                         | Example                                             |
| ---------------- | ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| **Web-level**    | Protect URLs                        | `/admin/**` accessible only to ADMIN                |
| **Method-level** | Annotate service/controller methods | `@PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")`                 |
| **Domain-level** | Object-based permissions            | Custom AccessDecisionVoter for fine-grained control |


| Annotation              | Purpose                                              |
| ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| `@EnableWebSecurity`    | Enables web security configuration                   |
| `@EnableMethodSecurity` | Allows method-level annotations like `@PreAuthorize` |
| `@PreAuthorize`         | Authorize access before method execution             |
| `@PostAuthorize`        | Authorize after method execution                     |
| `@Secured`              | Restrict access to specific roles                    |
| `@RolesAllowed`         | JSR-250 standard for roles                           |

Common Authentication Mechanism:
| Mechanism                | Description                                                 |
| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Basic Auth**           | Sends username/password with each request (Base64 encoded)  |
| **Form Login**           | Uses login page form submission                             |
| **JWT (JSON Web Token)** | Client stores token; server validates signature (stateless) |
| **OAuth2**               | Third-party login (Google, GitHub, etc.)                    |

For REST APIs → JWT is most common.

CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery)
Spring Security enables CSRF protection by default for form-based logins.
For REST APIs, CSRF can be disabled since APIs don’t use sessions or forms,because API uses stateless authentication with tokens, 
so CSRF protection is not required

http.csrf().disable();


Summary — Lead-Level Pointers

✅ Spring Security is filter-based → sits in front of DispatcherServlet
✅ Separates authentication (verify identity) and authorization (check access)
✅ Supports method-level and URL-level security
✅ UserDetailsService and PasswordEncoder are core interfaces
✅ For REST APIs → prefer JWT (stateless)
✅ For monolithic apps → session-based or form login
✅ Always explain your security choices and why you used them